A `.mtf` file sees the fixed `@html` and `@styles` helpers and values from helper files in the same module. It does not see module root declarations.

### Selecting a helper value

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- docs/settings.moth
    site_name #= "Moth"
]

[codeblock, $code "md":
    -- docs/intro.mtf
    # Welcome to [@settings site_name]
]

The path names the helper file and the symbol names one compile-time value. Keep them on one line. No `@mod.moth` file is needed.

The selected name does not become a local name. It cannot clash with a prelude helper.

### Root files stay private

A value written in `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` or `@entry.moth` is not visible to a `.mtf` file, even when the root exports it. Move shared values into an ordinary helper file and select them with a qualified reference.

### The fixed helpers

`.mtf` files see constants from `@html` and `@styles` without a dependency clause:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    [p: A paragraph.]
    [h1, center: A centred heading.]
]

That prelude comes from the official Core `@html` and `@styles` packages. It is available under every builder, not only HTML projects.

### A nested override

[codeblock, $code "text":
    [$raw: <div>Raw HTML here</div>]
]

Nested templates default to `$md`, but an explicit directive overrides that.

### Compile-time only

Everything in a `.mtf` body folds during compilation. A runtime value cannot be used there. The file produces one `content` string and makes no page fragment of its own.
